                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_579">Westminster, Middlesex.
                        Benedictine abbey of St Peter</orgName><p>Formerly at Sawley abbey,
                        identified by ex libris. Almost certainly the book seen at Westminster by
                        John Bale (1495--1563), who listed a series of rare and unique works, all
                        carried by this manuscript, in his Index, with the reference 'Ex coenobio
                        Westmonasteriensi' or 'Ex libro Westmonasteriensi' (Index, 13, 348, 407,
                        409, 419, 461). It is not known how the book came to be transferred to
                        Westminster, nor is it certain that Bale saw the book there before
                        dissolution of the house in 1540.</p><p>The manuscript was also very fully
                        described by a rather later writer in a Wrest Park manuscript (HMC 2nd
                        Report, no. 19) under the heading 'Ex quodam antiquo volumine
                        Westmonasteriensi'.</p><p>John Joscelyn wrote that Pekyns, prebendary of
                        Westminster, had Simeon of Durham and John and Richard of Hexham, probably
                        this manuscript. </p><p/></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Archbishop Matthew Parker (1504--1575), given
                     to him by Nicholas Wootton.</provenance>
               </history>
            </msDesc>
